      "role": "user",
      "content": "i hear you on the nuance part, but isn't there a bit of a double standard there? like, people always say \"innovation\" as a shield to protect these giants, but we don't hear that same \"innovation\" defense when old-school monopolies like Standard Oil or AT&T were being challenged. it feels like the \"innovation\" argument is just a way to say that being too big is actually a good thing, which seems kind of backwards if the goal is a fair market. do you think the tech sector gets a pass because of how much we rely on them now, or is there a legitimate reason to view their scale differently than the monopolies of the past?",
      "source": "evaluator"
